       ... 26 more; the whole table is in the archive (it is one line per zone)
    
    == the verdict
       window A was the ladder ALLOWED (the fix as installed); B was the ladder BLOCKED; C was allowed
       again. Busy: A 0.80, B 0.79, C 0.81 of 4 cores (a READING, not a gate: the ladder works
       on idle power, and this figure counts busy time, so the two are not expected to track).
       the largest warming from A to B on any tsens zone: 5.5 C  (thermal_zone9 (tsens_tz_sensor8))
       and from A to C (the control, same state as A):   0.0 C  (thermal_zone20 (tsens_tz_sensor19))
       the threshold this experiment calls a cost: 0.2 C -- two steps of the instrument's 0.1 C
    
       -> COST-MEASURED: blocking the ladder warmed the hottest tsens zone by 5.5 C (thermal_zone9 (tsens_tz_sensor8)), and
          putting it back did not leave that warming behind (C is 0.0 C above A). At this load, that is
          what the third heat cause is worth in temperature.
    
       Read it as a READING and not as the ladder's price in general: ambient is not controlled, the
       battery's charging state is not controlled, and the third window can only catch a drift that
       outlasts the second -- a drift that happens to peak in window B and vanish in C is indistinguishable
       from this effect HERE, and is exactly what the control cannot rule out. The zones are the
       instrument's numbers, normalised by it; this script does not divide anything.
    
       The parameter is N (/sys/module/lpm_levels/parameters/sleep_disabled) -- the state it started in.


SECTION 2 -- THE STATE AFTER THE RUN (read a minute later, same boot)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    boot_id:       2fbf9f8e-deea-4955-ad0c-6bddf0fb14f5   (unchanged -- no reboot)
    uptime:        8895.69 s at the read (8477.70 s before the run; the run took ~123 s)
    sleep_disabled: N        <- the INSTALLED state, restored by the script's own undo and re-proved
    download_mode:  0        <- the panic guard, still disarmed (refusal A's precondition)
    lpm unit:       active
    hottest tsens:  42.7 C   <- back in window A's range (42.2), against window B's 47.7

    The instrument's own words, from the run above: "wrote 0 and read 'N' back: the ladder is
    ALLOWED again -- this is the state it started in." and "final state: ... reads 'N' -- the fix is
    where this script found it." Both are read-back proofs, not promises.

    There is no --status after the run in this archive because the line above answers the same
    question from the device itself; the two other readings (download_mode, the unit) are the ones
    the run does not print.


SECTION 3 -- THE READING, AND WHAT IT IS A READING OF
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    the largest warming from A to B on any tsens zone:  5.5 C  (thermal_zone9, tsens_tz_sensor8)
    and from A to C (the control, same state as A):     0.0 C  (thermal_zone20, tsens_tz_sensor19)
    -> COST-MEASURED

    The whole tsens picture, which is the part worth reading rather than the single number:

      zone                       A      B      C     B-A    C-A
      tsens_tz_sensor8        42.2   47.7   40.9   +5.5   -1.3
      tsens_tz_sensor4        41.2   44.8   40.3   +3.6   -0.9
      tsens_tz_sensor6        41.2   44.5   39.9   +3.3   -1.3
      tsens_tz_sensor3        41.9   44.8   40.9   +2.9   -1.0
      tsens_tz_sensor5        41.9   44.5   41.2   +2.6   -0.7
      tsens_tz_sensor7        41.2   43.8   40.3   +2.6   -0.9
      tsens_tz_sensor19       40.4   40.4   40.4   +0.0   +0.0
      (six SoC sensors moved together and one did not move at all; the rails moved 0.5 C or less)

    THE CONTROL WENT THE OTHER WAY, and that is what makes this a reading rather than a coincidence:
    C is 0.7-1.3 C BELOW A on every sensor that B warmed. The phone was cooling during the run (the
    run is ~2 minutes of a phone that had been idle for two hours, and the ambient had been falling),
    and window B still landed 2.6-5.5 C above BOTH of the windows taken in A's state. So the effect
    is larger than the drift available to explain it AND opposite in sign to it.

    What this does NOT say: it is one run, on one boot, at one load, with ambient and charging
    uncontrolled. The third window can only catch a drift that outlasts window B; a drift that peaks
    inside B and vanishes by C is indistinguishable from this effect here. It does not say the
    ladder is worth 5.5 C at any other load -- at a busier load the SoC is warm for other reasons
    and the deep states may matter less, or more.
